## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform fixed-bit manipulations without loops, using SWAR-style packing to reduce 64 bits to 32 bits in one pass.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR parallel bit extraction
- Fixed-mask chain for bit packing
- No branching for deterministic performance

## Code Example

```c
uint32 compressEvenBits64(uint64 x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    return (uint32)x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to extract the odd bits instead of even bits?
- What are the trade-offs vs a loop-based approach on various architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
3D Morton codes enable fast spatial indexing in grid-like domains; a branchless, fixed-bit spreading approach scales predictably. 

## Key Concepts
- Morton codes for 3D require interleaving bits from three coordinates.
- Bit spreading (dilating) inserts two zero bits between each original bit; a fixed sequence of masks/shifts yields a branchless implementation.
- BigInt vs. Number in JavaScript: use BigInt for 64-bit results if implementing in JS; otherwise 64-bit types in C/C++ handle it natively.

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n) {
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn; // 21 bits
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n)) & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n)) & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n)) & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x, y, z) {
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify correctness for random inputs? 
- How to adapt to larger coordinates or alternative word sizes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform full-width bit reversal in constant time using SWAR patterns, not per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit composition as hi/lo 64-bit
- SWAR bit-reversal: 1,2,4,8,16,32-bit block swaps
- Final cross-half swap to complete reversal

## Code Example
```javascript
// 64-bit reversal helper
static uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}
uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to larger widths (256-bit) with minimal extra code?
- What intrinsics or compiler options help optimize this on typical CPUs?

Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about 128-bit values and apply bit-twiddling without branching. This requires cross-half logic and edge-case handling.

Key Concepts
- 128-bit decomposition into hi and lo
- a 64-bit value is a power of two if v & (v-1) == 0 and v != 0
- exactly one half nonzero implies overall power-of-two only if that half is itself a power of two

Code Example

```c
uint8_t isPowerOfTwo128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  // Exactly one half must be nonzero and that half must be a power of two
  int hiPow = (hi & (hi - 1)) == 0 && hi != 0;
  int loPow = (lo & (lo - 1)) == 0 && lo != 0;
  return ((hi == 0) ^ (lo == 0)) ? (hi ? hiPow : loPow) : 0;
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for a specific CPU architecture (e.g., using intrinsics)?
- How would you extend this to 256-bit values?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement bit-twiddling without loops, using SWAR patterns for constant-time population count.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (Small/Word-level Parallelism)
- 64-bit masks and carry-free sums
- No intrinsics or branches; deterministic performance

## Code Example
```javascript
uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to count bits in 128-bit values? 
- What are the trade-offs vs. hardware popcnt instructions in modern CPUs?

